<Window xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        xmlns:chrome="using:Avalonia.Controls.Chrome"
        xmlns:vm="using:TanMenu.Wpf.ViewModels"
        x:Class="TanMenu.Wpf.MainWindow"
        Title="TanMenu"
        Width="1000" Height="688" MinWidth="760" MinHeight="568"
        WindowStartupLocation="Manual"
        TransparencyLevelHint="Mica"
        Background="Transparent"
        Icon="/wwwroot/app.ico"
        FontSize="14"
        Foreground="{DynamicResource TextPrimaryBrush}"
        ExtendClientAreaToDecorationsHint="True"
        ExtendClientAreaTitleBarHeightHint="48">

  <!--
    The shell: a Mica window split into a title strip, a permanently icon-only navigation rail
    (48 DIP wide, see NavRailPane below) and a page host.

    Round 3, Task 2 (user items 4, 5) reshaped the rail to match Task Manager's OWN measured shape
    (live UI Automation + pixel geometry, not eyeballed):
    Task Manager's rail is not one flat list — its page rows (进程/性能/…/服务) sit in a top "*"-sized
    group and its Settings gear sits alone in a SEPARATE, bottom-pinned footer group, separated from the
    page list by whatever space is left over. This shell's own 应用/设置 map onto that same shape: 应用
    is the only real "page list" entry (NavRail, a ListBox in the Grid's "*" row), and 设置 — a real page
    here too, exactly like Task Manager's own Settings — moves into a second, bottom-pinned ListBox
    (NavFooterRail) alongside the four commands the title strip's old ⋯ overflow used to hold
    (打开主目录/刷新/关于/退出). Those four are NOT pages — invoking one leaves whatever page is
    currently shown alone — so they are plain Buttons (Theme="{StaticResource TmNavRowButton}"), not
    ListBoxItems: a Button has no :selected pseudoclass at all, which is what makes "a command row must
    never look selected" true by construction rather than by a style rule a future edit could defeat.

    Round 4, item 1 deleted the rail's expand capability outright — the hamburger toggle, the 200 DIP
    expanded width, the width animation, the six label TextBlocks and the state class that switched
    between the two widths are all gone. The rail is 48 DIP, icon-only, always; that is both
    Task Manager's own measured pane width and the only state this app ever shipped as its default.
    Each row's ToolTip.Tip and AutomationProperties.Name (ApplyRailText, code-behind) are therefore now
    the ONLY label any row carries, not a duplicate of a visible one.

    Round 4, item 2 reordered the footer to 退出 · separator · 关于 · 打开主目录 · 刷新 · 设置 and gave
    it its one group boundary. The parenthesised list above is the order the ⋯ overflow held, not the
    order these rows sit in now — see the footer StackPanel's own comment below for the current order
    and the reasoning behind it.

    ExtendClientAreaToDecorationsHint pulls the whole client area — including the strip where the OS
    used to paint its own title bar — under our control. ExtendClientAreaTitleBarHeightHint is pinned to
    48, the Windows 11 title bar height at 100% scaling, rather than -1 (OS default), so the strip below
    lines up with it exactly. Height/MinHeight are 48 taller than round 1's 640/520: extending the
    client area moves the strip's height OUT OF the OS-added frame and INTO the window's own client
    size, so without this the nav rail and page host would lose exactly the height the strip now
    occupies.

    CORRECTED FACT (Task 1 review, established by decompiling Avalonia 12.1 — see the round-2 plan's
    "CORRECTION" note): the caption buttons this hint turns on are NOT drawn by Windows. Setting
    ExtendClientAreaToDecorationsHint makes Avalonia instantiate its OWN WindowDrawnDecorations overlay,
    styled from the Fluent theme's default ControlTheme for that type — which draws the window title (a
    TextBlock inside PART_TitleTextPanel) and Close/Minimize/Maximize, PLUS a PART_FullScreenButton Task
    Manager never shows. Left unsuppressed, that overlay painted a second title on top of this strip's
    own and a fourth, non-Windows-11 caption button (both visible in the shipped Task 1 screenshots).
    WindowDecorationsTheme below overrides that default theme, based on the same Fluent one via BasedOn,
    to hide exactly the two parts this strip is a deliberate replacement for (the title panel and the
    fullscreen button) while leaving Minimize/Maximize/Close and all their built-in states untouched.
    Decompiling Avalonia.Themes.Fluent 12.1.0 (Task 2 review) confirms the template contains NO icon
    element at all — an earlier revision of this comment claimed it drew an icon, inferred from a
    screenshot rather than from the template itself, and that was wrong.

    The strip is marked WindowDecorationProperties.ElementRole="TitleBar" so dragging it moves the
    window; the search field is marked "User" so it takes pointer/keyboard input instead of starting a
    drag. CaptionSpacer reserves the room the three caption buttons need — see the literal Width on that
    Border for why it is a plain constant, not something read from Window.WindowDecorationMargin: that
    Thickness cannot answer the question at all on Win32 (confirmed by reading
    WindowImpl.UpdateExtendMargins — Left/Right/Bottom are hardcoded to 0.0, and Top merely echoes back
    ExtendClientAreaTitleBarHeightHint's own literal, carrying no information about the buttons or the
    real DPI).

    Round 3, Task 2: the ⋯ overflow button that used to sit here (打开主目录/刷新/关于/退出 behind a
    MenuFlyout) is gone — those four commands are now rows in the navigation rail below (NavRailContent's
    bottom-pinned footer group), so the search field is the only thing left in this middle group besides
    CaptionSpacer.

    The search box binds to AppPage's own LauncherViewModel (exposed as AppPage.ViewModel) rather than a
    second view model: AppPage still owns the keyboard model (Escape, Down, the tile grid) and
    constructs/disposes the one LauncherViewModel instance; the shell only hosts the search control that
    used to sit in AppPage's header and talks to the SAME instance, exactly as if the header had never
    moved. The rail's four relocated commands (OnOpenRootClick/OnRefreshClick/OnAboutClick/OnExitClick,
    code-behind) reach that same instance the same way. The one thing that still needs its own imperative
    binding, exactly as before, is the 字体 setting — see ApplyWindowFont in the code-behind.
  -->

  <Window.WindowDecorationsTheme>
    <ControlTheme TargetType="chrome:WindowDrawnDecorations"
                  BasedOn="{StaticResource {x:Type chrome:WindowDrawnDecorations}}">
      <!-- ══ Round 4, item 5: NOT here, and that is the finding ════════════════════════════════════
           The user's item 5 ("任务栏和左侧导航栏应该是背景色相同") turned out to be a real, opaque WHITE
           band painted across the client's top 48 DIP at full width — measured at (255,255,255) against
           the rail's (241,241,249) one row below it, at the same x, on a PrintWindow capture of the live
           build. It is painted by a Panel named PART_TitleBar in Avalonia's drawn-decorations UNDERLAY,
           from {DynamicResource TitleBarBackgroundBrush}.

           A rule HERE — "^ /template/ Panel#PART_TitleBar" with Background="Transparent" — was written
           first, because this ControlTheme is where every other override of that template lives, and it
           MEASURABLY DID NOT WORK: the part still reported bg=White and the band was still on screen.
           The selector is fine; an Opacity setter added to the same rule as a probe came back applied
           (op=0.5) on the same run. The template binds that Background on the part itself, and a style
           setter for the same property does not outrank it. Do not re-add such a rule here.
           What DOES work is overriding the resource key, in Themes/Fluent2/Tokens.axaml beside the three
           other Fluent template keys this app overrides — that file carries the full write-up, the
           decompile it came from and the before/after numbers. -->

      <!-- Both parts named here are Avalonia's own default drawn-decorations content, not ours — see
           the CORRECTED FACT note above. Ground-truthed against the LIVE running app (a temporary
           GetVisualDescendants() dump reached via TopLevelHost, the real internal Control that wraps
           this Window as ITS OWN VisualChild (which is why walking down from the Window itself does not
           reach WindowDrawnDecorations), not trusted from decompiled-XAML summaries alone:
           PART_TitleTextPanel and PART_FullScreenButton are both real, confirmed names.

           PART_FullScreenButton: Setter Property="IsVisible" works — Avalonia, unlike WPF's Hidden,
           drops an invisible element from layout entirely, so the caption-button StackPanel naturally
           shrinks to Minimize/Maximize/Close with no gap where Fullscreen was.

           PART_TitleTextPanel: IsVisible does NOT work the same way here — its own template declares
           IsVisible as a local TemplateBinding (to HasTitleBar, which is true whenever a title bar
           exists), and a local value always outranks a Style setter in Avalonia's value-precedence
           chain regardless of which ControlTheme declared the setter or in what order. Opacity has no
           such competing local binding, so it is what actually suppresses the panel's content — the
           panel still occupies its (otherwise invisible) layout slot in the OVERLAY layer, which is a
           separate compositing layer from this window's own content and therefore never affects this
           strip's own layout. -->
      <Style Selector="^ /template/ Panel#PART_TitleTextPanel">
        <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="^ /template/ Button#PART_FullScreenButton">
        <Setter Property="IsVisible" Value="False" />
      </Style>

      <!-- Round 3, Task 1 (user item 3, "右上角三个按钮位置有偏差"): the three real caption buttons sat
           floating in the middle of the 48 DIP band instead of hugging its top edge the way a native
           Windows 11 title bar's buttons do. Root cause, confirmed against Avalonia 12.1.0's actual
           Fluent theme source (github.com/AvaloniaUI/Avalonia, tag 12.1.0,
           src/Avalonia.Themes.Fluent/Controls/WindowDrawnDecorations.xaml): PART_OverlayPanel (the
           StackPanel holding these buttons) has Height bound to TemplateBinding TitleBarHeight — 48 in
           this app — but each button's own ControlTheme (FluentDrawnCaptionButton) sets a FIXED
           Width="45" Height="30" with VerticalAlignment="Stretch". Stretch cannot stretch past an
           explicitly fixed Height, and Avalonia's layout falls back to centering in that case, which
           is exactly what live measurement showed before this fix: Minimize's own
           BoundingRectangle sat inset ~14 physical px (~9.3 DIP) from the panel's top and ~13 physical px
           (~8.7 DIP) from its bottom, at 150% scale, measured before this fix. Windows'
           own native caption buttons never float like that — they sit flush against the outer edge of
           whatever title bar hosts them, regardless of that bar's height. Overriding VerticalAlignment to
           Top on the three real buttons (Fullscreen is already suppressed above, so it needs no rule)
           moves them flush to Y=0 while leaving their native 45x30 footprint — and therefore their hit
           target and click behaviour — completely untouched.

           CORRECTION (Task 1 review): an earlier revision of this comment justified the override by
           pointing at PART_TitleTextPanel/PART_FullScreenButton above as "the same technique" already
           proven in this file. That overstated it — neither of those two also has ITS OWN ControlTheme
           setting the SAME property (Opacity, IsVisible) the way FluentDrawnCaptionButton's own
           VerticalAlignment="Stretch" Setter competes with this one, so neither actually exercised a
           same-property conflict against a part's own default theme. What actually makes a Style
           declared HERE (inside WindowDecorationsTheme, itself BasedOn Fluent's own
           WindowDrawnDecorations ControlTheme) win over FluentDrawnCaptionButton's own Setter for the
           same property on the same part is Avalonia's style/theme FRAME precedence: a control's own
           local Theme override (this one, assigned via Window.WindowDecorationsTheme) is applied at a
           higher-priority frame than the separate ControlTheme a nested template part resolves for
           itself — confirmed by the reviewer decompiling that precedence ranking, and by this fix's own
           live, reproduced result (below), not re-derived here from first principles. -->
      <Style Selector="^ /template/ Button#PART_MinimizeButton">
        <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Top" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="^ /template/ Button#PART_MaximizeButton">
        <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Top" />
      </Style>
      <Style Selector="^ /template/ Button#PART_CloseButton">
        <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Top" />
      </Style>
    </ControlTheme>
  </Window.WindowDecorationsTheme>

  <Grid RowDefinitions="48,*">

    <!-- ══ Title strip: app icon + name, search, overflow, then room for the caption buttons ══════ -->
    <Border Grid.Row="0"
            Background="Transparent"
            chrome:WindowDecorationProperties.ElementRole="TitleBar">
      <!-- Round 3, Task 1 review fix: "*,Auto,*" rather than the old "Auto,Auto,*,Auto,Auto,Auto"
           (which had exactly ONE flexible column, before the search field and none after it — the
           field was pinned at a fixed offset from the right edge, not centred, measured at review as
           ~69% of client width against Task Manager's own ~48.75%). Two EQUAL-weight star columns
           flank a single Auto column that sizes to SearchField's own (now dynamic — see
           ApplySearchFieldWidth in the code-behind) width, and Avalonia's Grid splits leftover space
           between equal-weight star columns evenly — which is what centres that Auto column on the
           WHOLE window, not just on whatever happens to sit to either side of it, as long as neither
           side's content is wide enough to eat into the other star column's share. Confirmed by
           measurement after the change, at multiple window widths, not only by this construction
           argument. -->
      <Grid x:Name="TitleStripContent"
            x:DataType="vm:LauncherViewModel"
            ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto,*">

        <!-- Left star: icon + name, pinned to the LEFT of their own column so widening the window
             (which widens both star columns equally) cannot drag them rightward with it. -->
        <StackPanel Grid.Column="0"
                    Orientation="Horizontal"
                    HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                    Margin="16,0,0,0">
          <Image Width="20" Height="20"
                 VerticalAlignment="Center"
                 Source="avares://TanMenu.Wpf/wwwroot/app-tile.png" />
          <TextBlock Text="TanMenu"
                     Margin="10,0,0,0"
                     VerticalAlignment="Center" />
        </StackPanel>

        <!-- Width="280" here is only the value on screen for the instant between this control's
             creation and the first ApplySearchFieldWidth() call (code-behind) — see that method's own
             remarks for why a fixed width was wrong (review finding, Round 3 Task 1) and what replaces
             it: the field's real, live width tracks the window's ClientSize, proportionally, the way
             Task Manager's own measurably does. -->
        <Border Grid.Column="1"
                x:Name="SearchField"
                Classes="tm-searchfield"
                chrome:WindowDecorationProperties.ElementRole="User"
                Width="280"
                Margin="12,0"
                VerticalAlignment="Center">
          <Panel>
            <DockPanel>
              <TextBlock DockPanel.Dock="Left" Classes="tm-icon subtle small"
                         Text="&#xE721;" Margin="0,0,8,0" />
              <!-- Background and BorderThickness are LOCAL values on purpose: a local value outranks a
                   style setter for the SAME property on the SAME element, which is what stops
                   Avalonia's own :pointerover TextBox styling from repainting a field the Border
                   already paints. CORRECTION (Round 3, Task 1): this does NOT also block the built-in
                   ":focus" styling — that one targets Border#PART_BorderElement, a DIFFERENT element
                   living inside TextBox's own control template, which carries no local value of its
                   own regardless of what is set here. See Launcher.axaml's
                   "TextBox.tm-search:focus /template/ Border#PART_BorderElement" rule for what
                   actually suppresses it, and why. -->
              <TextBox x:Name="SearchBox"
                       Classes="tm-search"
                       AutomationProperties.Name="{Binding SearchPlaceholderText}"
                       Background="Transparent"
                       BorderThickness="0"
                       PlaceholderText="{Binding SearchPlaceholderText}"
                       Text="{Binding Filter, Mode=TwoWay}"
                       KeyDown="OnSearchKeyDown"
                       GotFocus="OnSearchFocusChanged"
                       LostFocus="OnSearchFocusChanged" />
            </DockPanel>
            <!-- Windows 11's focused-TextBox accent underline (Launcher.axaml's own remarks on
                 Border.tm-searchfield.focused explain why this is a second element rather than a
                 BorderBrush change): transparent at rest, painted only while SearchField carries the
                 "focused" class. IsHitTestVisible="False" keeps it from ever stealing a click from the
                 TextBox it sits on top of — it draws over the DockPanel only because it is the LATER
                 child in this Panel, not because it needs input. -->
            <Border Classes="tm-searchfield-focusbar"
                    IsHitTestVisible="False"
                    HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"
                    VerticalAlignment="Bottom"
                    Height="2"
                    Margin="1,0,1,1"
                    CornerRadius="0,0,3,3" />
          </Panel>
        </Border>

        <!-- Right star: caption reservation only, pinned to the RIGHT of its own column for the same
             reason the left group is pinned left. Round 3, Task 2 removed OverflowButton (and its
             MenuFlyout) from here entirely — 打开主目录/刷新/关于/退出 are now rows in the navigation
             rail's bottom-pinned footer (see the Grid.Row="1" section below) — leaving CaptionSpacer as
             this StackPanel's only remaining child. It stays a StackPanel rather than collapsing back to
             a bare Border directly in Grid.Column="2": a future right-aligned addition to this group
             needs no structural change, exactly the property this shape was already kept for. -->
        <StackPanel Grid.Column="2"
                    Orientation="Horizontal"
                    HorizontalAlignment="Right">

          <!-- A PLAIN CONSTANT, stated as one rather than disguised as one (see the CORRECTED FACT note
               above for why nothing in Window's own API can measure this on Win32). It does not need to
               react to DPI: Minimize/Maximize/Close are fixed-size (45x30 DIP each, confirmed live via a
               temporary GetVisualDescendants() dump of the Fluent theme's own WindowDrawnDecorations
               template) in Avalonia's own LOGICAL coordinate space, not a Win32 system metric — a DIP
               measurement is exact at every real monitor scale, not an approximation that happens to work
               at 150%. 140 is the three-button cluster's OWN measured on-screen width (UI Automation,
               confirmed identically at two different window sizes after PART_FullScreenButton was
               suppressed above), not 3 x 45 = 135: the cluster carries a few extra DIP of edge padding
               the per-button figure alone doesn't account for. -->
          <Border x:Name="CaptionSpacer" Width="140" Background="Transparent" />
        </StackPanel>

      </Grid>
    </Border>

    <!-- Grid.Column="0" is "Auto", not a literal 48: NavRailPane below carries its OWN explicit Width
         and the Auto column just follows whatever that Border measures at, so the rail's width is
         stated exactly once in this file (Round 4, item 1 removed the second state that Width used to
         animate between; a fixed column here would restore a second copy of the number instead). -->
    <Grid Grid.Row="1" ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*">

      <!-- ══ Navigation rail pane: 48 DIP, icon-only, permanently ═══════════════════════════════════
           Round 4, item 1: the hamburger toggle, the 200 DIP expanded width and the DoubleTransition
           that animated between the two are deleted — a rail with one width needs neither a transition
           on that width nor the ClipToBounds that only existed to clip the animation. 48 DIP is Task
           Manager's own pane width, measured live (its PaneRoot is 72 physical px = 48 DIP at this
           machine's 150% scale) and the width this app has always opened at.

           This literal Width is the SINGLE source of truth for the rail's width. MainWindow.axaml.cs's
           RailWidthDip mirrors it as the window content fit's chrome term and has to be kept in step
           with it — see that constant's own remarks. -->
      <Border Grid.Column="0"
              x:Name="NavRailPane"
              Width="48"
              Background="Transparent">

        <!-- Two rows: Task Manager's own rail (measured, not eyeballed — its NavigationMenu is a Grid
             of exactly this shape) keeps its page list in a "*" row and pins Settings alone in a
             separate Auto row below it, the "*" row's own leftover space pushing the footer down to the
             pane's bottom edge. Round 4, item 1 removed the third (Auto) row this Grid used to open
             with, which held nothing but the hamburger toggle; nothing replaces it, so 应用 rises by
             that row's whole height. Classes="tm-nav-rail" is kept as this element's own name for
             anything that needs to scope a rule to the rail root — the three rail-state selectors that
             used to be its only readers went with the expanded state, so as of item 1 no Launcher.axaml
             selector keys off it. -->
        <Grid x:Name="NavRailContent" Classes="tm-nav-rail" RowDefinitions="*,Auto">

          <!-- Page list: 应用 only. 设置 is a real page too (PageHost.Content still swaps between the
               two exactly as before — see ShowPage), but it now lives in NavFooterRail below, pinned to
               the pane's bottom footer the same way Task Manager pins its own Settings gear, separated
               from this list. Padding is "0,12" — ZERO horizontal, unlike a plain ListBox default —
               because ListBoxItem's own Margin="4,2" (Launcher.axaml) already supplies the row's outer
               gutter; adding a second, ListBox-level horizontal padding on top of that (the pre-Task-2
               shape) narrowed the item to 32 DIP against Task Manager's OWN measured 40 DIP —
               confirmed by live UI Automation on both apps. Removing it is what makes
               the item column exactly 48 (pane) − 2×4 (item margin) = 40 DIP, matching
               Task Manager's row width with no other change. Grid.Row="0" since Round 4, item 1 deleted
               the hamburger row above it. -->
          <ListBox x:Name="NavRail"
                   Grid.Row="0"
                   Classes="tm-nav"
                   SelectedIndex="0"
                   SelectionChanged="OnNavSelectionChanged"
                   Background="Transparent"
                   BorderThickness="0"
                   Padding="0,12">
            <!-- Every rail icon TextBlock in this file carries a local Width="16": Task Manager's own
                 icon glyphs measure a FIXED, IDENTICAL 16 DIP box at the same X on every single row
                 (live UI Automation on 7 different glyphs — folder, chart, clock, wrench, people, list,
                 puzzle — all report x=5322), which only happens if the icon sits in a
                 fixed-size container rather than being centred by its own raw glyph metrics. Segoe
                 Fluent Icons glyphs do NOT all share the same advance width at a given FontSize — before
                 this fix, "应用"'s grid glyph and "设置"'s gear glyph centred 4 physical px apart from
                 each other (measured) purely from font-metric differences between the two characters,
                 even though both rows' content was correctly centred. A local value beats the tm-icon
                 style's own Setters (the same precedence this file already relies on elsewhere), and 16
                 DIP is not a round guess: it is exactly Task Manager's OWN measured icon box, and it also
                 exactly fills this row's own content width (40 DIP item − 2×12 DIP
                 ListBoxItem Padding, Launcher.axaml, = 16 DIP) with no slack on either side.

                 Round 4, item 1: the icon is now every row's WHOLE content — the label TextBlock and the
                 horizontal StackPanel that used to pair with it are gone, since the rail can no longer
                 widen to show a label. Centring is stated LOCALLY on the icon (tm-icon's own Setters say
                 the same thing, and a local value simply outranks them): it used to come from a pair of
                 rail-state rules in Launcher.axaml that centred the row's StackPanel, and item 1 deleted
                 both along with the StackPanels they targeted. -->
            <ListBoxItem x:Name="NavAppsItem">
              <TextBlock Classes="tm-icon" Width="16" Text="&#xE71D;"
                         HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
            </ListBoxItem>
          </ListBox>

          <!-- Bottom-pinned footer: Round 3, Task 2 (user item 4) moves the title strip's old ⋯
               overflow flyout (打开主目录/刷新/关于/退出) into the rail, here — and user item 5 asks to
               put 设置 where Task Manager's OWN Settings gear sits, separated from the page list. Both
               land in the SAME Auto row (Grid.Row="1" since Round 4, item 1 deleted the hamburger row
               this Grid used to open with): Row 0's "*" already gives the page list every
               pixel it does not use, which is what pushes this whole group to the pane's true bottom
               edge without this row needing an explicit height of its own — the identical mechanism
               Task Manager's own FooterMenuItemsHost relies on (measured: its MenuItemsScrollViewer
               fills a "*"-sized 1390 physical px even though its 7 items only occupy 414 of them).

               The four commands are Buttons (Theme="{StaticResource TmNavRowButton}"), not
               ListBoxItems, on purpose: they are one-shot actions that leave whatever page is on screen
               alone, never a destination the window navigates TO and stays on the way 应用/设置 are, and
               a Button has no :selected pseudoclass at all — so "a command row must never look
               selected" (the task's own constraint) holds by construction, not by a style rule a later
               edit could accidentally defeat. This also means they need no participation in
               OnNavSelectionChanged's selection bookkeeping below; Click is the whole contract.

               Folding all four into one flyout-triggering row instead was the other option this task
               named — rejected because nothing in the measured reference shows that shape anywhere:
               Task Manager's OWN rail (both its 7-item page list and its 1-item footer) is built
               entirely from flat, one-row-per-destination entries, never a row that reveals a flyout of
               further choices. Such a row would also just relocate the exact ⋯-button pattern
               this task explicitly removes, rather than actually removing it.

               Round 4, item 1: each row's content is now its icon alone — see NavAppsItem above for why
               the label TextBlocks and their StackPanels are gone and where the centring moved to. The
               row's ToolTip.Tip and AutomationProperties.Name (ApplyRailText) are its only label.
               Classes="tm-nav-row" below is kept for the same reason NavRailContent keeps
               Classes="tm-nav-rail" — as these rows' own name for anything that needs to scope a rule
               to them — but as of item 1 NO Launcher.axaml selector keys off it either: its only reader
               was one of the three rail-state rules item 1 deleted.

               ══ Round 4, item 2: the order below, and why it is what it is ═══════════════════════
               Top to bottom: 退出 · separator · 关于 · 打开主目录 · 刷新 · 设置. The rows are LITERAL
               XAML, deliberately: rebuilding the footer from a collection would need a DataTemplate
               over a type that does not exist, and every DataTemplate in this app must carry an
               x:DataType (AvaloniaUseCompiledBindingsByDefault + PublishAot, TanMenu.Wpf.csproj), so
               a reorder is an edit HERE and nowhere else. Nothing downstream is index-based —
               OnNavSelectionChanged branches on ReferenceEquals against NavFooterRail and ApplyRailText
               addresses every row by x:Name — so moving a row is inert by construction.

               退出 goes FIRST, away from 设置, because it fires immediately with no confirmation
               (ShellCommands.Exit's own comment: "there is no dialog and nothing to wait for"), and
               设置 is the row the app itself treats as most-reached — it is the only footer action with
               TWO other entry points (the tray menu, wired in App.axaml.cs to TrayService; and the 应用
               empty state's 打开设置… button, AppPage.axaml). A terminal action directly above the row
               the pointer returns to at a fixed screen coordinate is the worst adjacency this footer
               can have, and before this item it was exactly the adjacency it had.

               关于 buffers it, because it is the one footer action reachable NOWHERE else:
               LauncherViewModel.OpenAbout has exactly one caller, OnAboutClick. Every other row has a
               second route — 退出 on the tray menu; 刷新 next to 打开设置… in the 应用 empty state
               (AppPage.axaml, the "tm-empty" panel); 打开主目录 as 设置 → 文件夹 → 主文件夹's own open
               button (FoldersCard.axaml.cs — a different code path onto the same folder, not a call
               into LauncherViewModel.OpenRoot, which OnOpenRootClick below is the sole caller of). So
               关于 is the row that cannot be dropped and the one whose position costs the least.

               设置 stays bottom-most, matching the reference: Task Manager's rail footer holds exactly
               ONE entry, its Settings gear, pinned to the very bottom of the pane. The four commands
               being in the rail at all is this app's own divergence from that reference and was not
               re-litigated by this item.

               The separator is not decoration. The flat run of five was an accident of the Round 3
               move: the ⋯ overflow MenuFlyout it came from encoded THREE groups —
               [打开主目录, 刷新] | [关于] | [退出] — and both <Separator/> elements were dropped in
               transit. Exactly one is restored, and only the boundary that carries safety meaning
               (terminal vs reversible). NOTE that a menu convention (退出 last, as this app's own tray
               menu still does) is being deliberately overruled here: a tray menu is a transient popup
               that appears under the cursor and is gone after one click, whereas the rail is
               persistent chrome the user aims at repeatedly at a fixed coordinate. -->
          <StackPanel Grid.Row="1">
            <Button x:Name="NavExitItem"
                    Theme="{StaticResource TmNavRowButton}"
                    Classes="tm-nav-row"
                    Click="OnExitClick">
              <TextBlock Classes="tm-icon" Width="16" Text="&#xE7E8;"
                         HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
            </Button>

            <!-- The one group boundary, between the terminal action above and the reversible ones
                 below. Geometry is in Launcher.axaml's "Border.tm-nav-separator" rule; its Margin is
                 what makes the gap on either side of the hairline (6 DIP row-box to line) read as a
                 boundary against the 4 DIP that separates two ordinary rows. -->
            <Border Classes="tm-nav-separator" />

            <Button x:Name="NavAboutItem"
                    Theme="{StaticResource TmNavRowButton}"
                    Classes="tm-nav-row"
                    Click="OnAboutClick">
              <TextBlock Classes="tm-icon" Width="16" Text="&#xE946;"
                         HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
            </Button>
            <Button x:Name="NavOpenRootItem"
                    Theme="{StaticResource TmNavRowButton}"
                    Classes="tm-nav-row"
                    Click="OnOpenRootClick">
              <TextBlock Classes="tm-icon" Width="16" Text="&#xE8B7;"
                         HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
            </Button>
            <Button x:Name="NavRefreshItem"
                    Theme="{StaticResource TmNavRowButton}"
                    Classes="tm-nav-row"
                    Click="OnRefreshClick">
              <TextBlock Classes="tm-icon" Width="16" Text="&#xE72C;"
                         HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
            </Button>

            <!-- 设置: a real page (see ShowPage), styled and measured exactly like NavRail's own
                 NavAppsItem (same "tm-nav" ListBox class, same ListBoxItem shape) so its selected
                 treatment — Task Manager's own left accent bar + subtle rounded fill, confirmed by
                 pixel-sampling a live screenshot of Task Manager's selected row: a
                 ~3.3 DIP accent-coloured bar flush to the item's left edge, and a fill spanning the
                 REST of the item's own bounds edge-to-edge, both already exactly what
                 "ListBox.tm-nav ListBoxItem:selected" (Launcher.axaml) already painted before this
                 task — reads identically to 应用's own. Padding="0,0,0,4" (not the "0,12" NavRail uses)
                 leaves no top inset of its own — the command row directly above (NavRefreshItem since
                 Round 4, item 2 reordered this footer; NavExitItem before it) carries Margin="4,2" and
                 its bottom half already supplies that gap — and a small 4 DIP bottom inset from the
                 pane's true bottom edge, matching Task Manager's own measured ~4 DIP gap between its
                 FooterMenuItemsHost and the window edge.

                 This ListBox stays the LAST child of the footer StackPanel, and the "0,0,0,4" padding
                 above is the encoding of that: it is the only element here that insets itself from the
                 pane's bottom edge. Round 4, item 2 chose an order that keeps it last for exactly that
                 reason — any order that moved 设置 up would have to unpick this padding too. -->
            <ListBox x:Name="NavFooterRail"
                     Classes="tm-nav"
                     SelectionChanged="OnNavSelectionChanged"
                     Background="Transparent"
                     BorderThickness="0"
                     Padding="0,0,0,4">
              <ListBoxItem x:Name="NavSettingsItem">
                <TextBlock Classes="tm-icon" Width="16" Text="&#xE713;"
                           HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              </ListBoxItem>
            </ListBox>
          </StackPanel>
        </Grid>
      </Border>

      <!-- ══ Content pane: a solid surface, not Mica ════════════════════════════════════════════════
           Task Manager's shape: the backdrop shows through the title strip and the nav rail, but the
           page underneath them is an opaque layer. Neither page paints an opaque surface of its own —
           SettingsPage's root ScrollViewer sets no Background at all and AppPage's root Panel sets only
           Transparent — which is what lets this single Border stand in for the solid pane under EITHER
           page instead of each page having to paint it separately. (An earlier revision of this comment
           said both were "left with no background of their own"; AppPage's root does carry
           Background="Transparent", so that was half wrong.)

           Round 4, item 5 gave the two pages two DIFFERENT looks on this one Border, so it is no longer
           "one opaque surface colour" — an earlier revision of this comment argued at length that
           reusing SurfaceBaseBrush here kept that phrase meaning exactly one thing, and that is now
           false in both halves. 应用 gets a pure white pane with a 1 DIP top+left hairline and an 8 DIP
           top-left corner (all four values measured off the user's Task Manager reference); 设置 keeps
           SurfaceBaseBrush, flat and borderless, because that is what the SAME reference does on ITS
           settings page and because our settings cards would otherwise composite to the exact colour of
           a white page. ShowPageCore (code-behind) toggles the "settings" class; the measurements, the
           arithmetic and the reason both looks are STYLES rather than local attributes here all live in
           Launcher.axaml's "Border.tm-page-pane" block.

           ClipToBounds is defensive. Whether Avalonia's Border clips its child to the CORNER RADIUS as
           opposed to the bounds rectangle was not verified, and nothing here depends on it: on 应用 the
           tile body is inset 16 DIP by SurfacePadding (the Body StackPanel's Margin, Views/AppPage.axaml)
           and the nearest thing to the corner is a docked notice at Margin="12,0,12,8" — both clear the
           8 DIP arc horizontally — and 设置 has no radius at all under the .settings rule. -->
      <Border Grid.Column="1"
              x:Name="PagePane"
              Classes="tm-page-pane"
              ClipToBounds="True">
        <ContentControl x:Name="PageHost" />
      </Border>
    </Grid>
  </Grid>
</Window>
